{
  "abstract": "We prove two restrictions on planar decompositions of dense clique interfaces. Let a private five-clique be complete to three centers and to an outer clique of order at least two. If the centers and an outer vertex are cofacial in one layer, then in each layer some interior component has a neighbor at every center. With three outer vertices, such a component must exist in at least one layer even without a cofaciality assumption. The arguments combine movable branches, classical nonbiplanar completions, and compact triangulation certificates. The remaining finite cases comprise 9,064 exposed exceptional interfaces and 299 large-arm interfaces; all have independently checked Kuratowski subdivisions. A related 2,001-case certificate excludes a common facial five-set in both layers of a complete graph on eight vertices, while literal rotations verify adjacent positive cases. We apply these restrictions to the central-edge deletion of a specified 25-vertex clique blow-up from a counting-class construction. Disconnected-interior normal forms and outside-component constraints yield a necessary Boolean formula with a checked 21,077-clause unsatisfiable core. Both RUP and LRAT replay verify the retained proof. The supplement contains exact compact inputs, the proof, clause-membership audit, and standard-library subdivision checkers. Completeness of the nine- and eleven-vertex plantri triangulation lists and an earlier small nonbiplanar obstruction remain explicit imports. The results concern arbitrary representations of these dense interfaces and exclude one proposed equality carrier; four other deletion types and the Earth–Moon problem remain unresolved.",
